Sérac-Skronk
Ingredients: SOUL JAZZ / JAZZ-FUNK + TRIP HOP + REBEL, ANGRY & AGGRESSIVE + Tenor Saxophone
Creative Catalyst: The furious, hyper-pressurized acoustics of melting glacier caves, captured as screaming subterranean resonance and catastrophic ice-shelf calving.

Sérac-Skronk slams the greasy, pocket-locked organ vamps and slurred wah-bass of 1970s jazz-funk headfirst into the suffocating, down-tempo dread of Bristol trip-hop. This volatile rhythmic bedrock is detonated by a weaponized tenor saxophone played with violent embouchure through rusted overdrive pedals. The tracks breathe with the acoustic dread of melting permafrost: cracking shelf reverbs, sub-bass rumbles tuned to the resonant frequencies of hollowed ice chambers, and snapping air-bubble transients that burst across the mix like gunfire.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 68–76 BPM. A dragged-back, molasses-thick swing where the snare hits late with the weight of wet snow, underpinned by syncopated slap-bass grooves that stumble intentionally against unquantized trip-hop breakbeats.
- Harmonic Palette: Dark minor blues scales and dissonant diminished runs spiked with microtonal slurs. Filthy Hammond organ chords clash against piercing, multi-phonic saxophone overtones that simulate the groaning tension of splitting ice sheets.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Cryo-Convective Reverb: Impulse responses captured inside subglacial meltwater caves, creating decaying, booming reflections that swallow horn solos whole.
  2. Hydro-Preamp Redlining: The tenor saxophone bell is miked with submerged hydrophones dipped into slush and run through blown-out tube preamps to produce an aggressive, bubbling rasp.
  3. Bergy-Seltzer Sidechaining: Tectonic 808 sub-bass frequencies are dynamically triggered and modulated by the popping crackle of pressurized air escaping compressed glacier ice.
- Dynamics: Deceptive, claustrophobic quiet lulls listeners into an eerie false security before tectonic crescendos obliterate the mix—erupting into furious horn screams, distorted drum breaks, and roaring meltwater feedback.

Scene & Origin Lore
Sérac-Skronk was birthed in the subglacial runoff tunnels beneath the retreating Vatnajökull ice cap during the illicit polar sound protests of 2028. A clandestine collective of radical glaciologists, climate saboteurs, and exiled jazz instrumentalists—known as the Thaw-Rakes—hauled vintage tube amps, modified Farfisa organs, and battery-powered samplers into condemned ice grottos. Dressed in grease-streaked, thermal-reflective parkas lined with crampon spikes and welding goggles, the movement turned music into an acoustic demolition ritual. They played directly into the ice faces at earsplitting volumes, intentionally timing their dirtiest funk breakdowns to trigger micro-calvings from the ceilings. To the Thaw-Rakes, every blistering sax cry is a declaration of resistance against the industrial machine that boiled their world, using the dying acoustics of the glaciers as both their amplifier and their guillotine.

Typical Instruments
- Central Sonic Lead: Tenor Saxophone (run through Electro-Harmonix Big Muff fuzz, Crybaby wah, and water-submerged hydrophones).
- Keyboards & Synths: Hammond B3 Organ with a battered Leslie speaker, detuned Fender Rhodes electric piano, Mellotron (playing warped brass tapes).
- Rhythm Section: Custom Drop-C electric slap bass, vintage Akai MPC60 playing distorted live-drum breakbeats, Roland TR-808 sub-bass.
- Atmospheric Exotics: Hydrophone arrays recording live glacial drip-rhythms, fractured sheet-metal percussion, amplified ice chimes.

